# Line endings are consensus here, not preference.
#
# core/replay.mjs reports EDITION as the sha256 of its own bytes, every epoch
# block seals that digest, and the browser is served the same file so a reader
# can check the copy they were handed against the copy that computed the state.
# All three of those break if the bytes differ — and on Windows they did:
# core.autocrlf rewrote the working tree to CRLF while the published site carried
# LF, so the same file hashed two ways. 366 bytes of difference in one module,
# and the chain would have reported edition drift forever without either copy
# being wrong about anything a human could see.
#
# So text is LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on every platform.
# This is not a style rule; it is the rule that makes "the same file" true.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Bytes that are bytes. Never touched, never normalised.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.gz binary
*.car binary
*.ots binary

# The act log and the sealed chain are hashed line by line. A newline rewritten
# here is a root that no longer verifies.
*.jsonl -text
